      "description": "Internship and Career Services < Institute of American Indian Arts (IAIA) Internship and Career Services < Institute of American Indian Arts (IAIA) University Academic Affairs Library Academic Calendars Alpha Chi Balzer Gallery Course Schedule Digital Dome Indigenous Assessment Model Online Learning President’s List Registrar Academic Programs Broadcast Journalism (Cert) Business and Entrepreneurship (Cert) Museum Studies (Cert) Native American Art History (Cert) Cinematic Arts & Technology Computer Science Creative Writing Indigenous Liberal Studies Museum Studies Performing Arts Studio Arts Creative Writing (MFA) Cultural Administration (MFA) Studio Arts (MFA) Admissions Apply Catalog Meet our Recruiters Tuition and Fees Financial Aid Student Accounts Orientation New Freshman Summer Bridge “15 to Finish” Graduation Plan Dual Credit Student Affairs Activities Associated Student Government Campus Bookstore Café Bon Appétit Fitness and Wellness For Parents Forms Housing Pathways Council Vote Student Affairs Counseling Student Employment Internship and Career Services Early Support Disability Support Placement Testing Learning Lab Outreach Continuing Education Land-Grant Catalog Alum Museum Exhibitions Virtual Exhibitions Traveling Exhibitions Collection Education Happenings Join & Give Store Research Center Research Center Artist-in-Residence (A-i-R) Artists Scholars Happenings Journal Archives Access to Museum Collection Forms About Making History Mission and History President’s Message Board of Trustees President’s Cabinet Employment Institutional Research Weather and Livestream Communications Community News Events and Calendar Newsletter Photographs Style Guide Templates Campus Visit Campus Safety Weather and Livestream Open House Practices, Policy, and Accreditation Title IX Right to Know Freedom of Information Act Novel Coronavirus (COVID-19) Support IAIA Support Annual Benefit & Auction Give IAIA Foundation Planned Giving President’s Circle | Apply Portal Contact Give Happs Visit Select Page University Academic Affairs Library Academic Calendars Alpha Chi Balzer Gallery Course Schedule Digital Dome Indigenous Assessment Model Online Learning President’s List Registrar Academic Programs Broadcast Journalism (Cert) Business and Entrepreneurship (Cert) Museum Studies (Cert) Native American Art History (Cert) Cinematic Arts & Technology Computer Science Creative Writing Indigenous Liberal Studies Museum Studies Performing Arts Studio Arts Creative Writing (MFA) Cultural Administration (MFA) Studio Arts (MFA) Admissions Apply Catalog Meet our Recruiters Tuition and Fees Financial Aid Student Accounts Orientation New Freshman Summer Bridge “15 to Finish” Graduation Plan Dual Credit Student Affairs Activities Associated Student Government Campus Bookstore Café Bon Appétit Fitness and Wellness For Parents Forms Housing Pathways Council Vote Student Affairs Counseling Student Employment Internship and Career Services Early Support Disability Support Placement Testing Learning Lab Outreach Continuing Education Land-Grant Catalog Alum Museum Exhibitions Virtual Exhibitions Traveling Exhibitions Collection Education Happenings Join & Give Store Research Center Research Center Artist-in-Residence (A-i-R) Artists Scholars Happenings Journal Archives Access to Museum Collection Forms About Making History Mission and History President’s Message Board of Trustees President’s Cabinet Employment Institutional Research Weather and Livestream Communications Community News Events and Calendar Newsletter Photographs Style Guide Templates Campus Visit Campus Safety Weather and Livestream Open House Practices, Policy, and Accreditation Title IX Right to Know Freedom of Information Act Novel Coronavirus (COVID-19) Support IAIA Support Annual Benefit & Auction Give IAIA Foundation Planned Giving President’s Circle | Apply Portal Contact Give Happs Visit Student Success Center Student Success Financial Aid Student Affairs Counseling Student Success Advisors Mentorship Program Student Employment Internship & Career Early Support Disability Placement Testing Student Accounts Learning Lab The Pantry Food and Community Nights Student Affairs Counseling Student Success Advisors Mentorship Program Student Employment Internship & Career Early Support Disability Placement Testing Student Accounts Learning Lab The Pantry Food and Community Nights Home Student Success Center Internship and Career Services Internship and Career Services IAIA Student Jonathan Loretto (Cochiti Pueblo) IAIA Internship and Career Services provides a variety of free services to current students and alumni promoting student readiness for professional careers through web-based employment searches and present-day approaches to marketing yourself. Services include online career assessment, internships, resume building and resume editing. Career Career fairs Graduate school preparation Interviewing strategies Job search and strategies Off campus job postings Resume and cover letter writing assistance Internship A meaningful internship (for credit and non-credit) experience provides students with relevant workplace skills and networking opportunities that can put career goals in reach. An internship or apprenticeship enliven and enrich the aspects of learning, deepen understanding, and promote proficiency. Administered at IAIA, there are two paid internship programs students may be eligible for, Ascendium (formerly Great Lakes) and Journey Home. Internships place students within existing organizations to learn directly from employees, volunteers, clients, and others. Internships provide valuable hands-on real world opportunities for deepening student learning, and prepare students for professional careers in their chosen fields. With the approval of advisors, internships and apprenticeship are available. They can count as an elective or major credit toward graduation, although they do not count toward general education. IAIA includes Internships and Apprenticeships in its educational model. Students may enroll in these educational opportunities with the support of their academic advisors. Internships and apprenticeships may count for either elective or major credit to fulfill the requirements for graduation. They do not count toward general education. For internships and apprenticeships, contracts are written which specify the expectations for both the student and the supervisor or mentor. They list the total number of contact hours which will determine the amount of academic credit awarded. Contracts will state the days, times, and places where the learning will occur. Contracts are signed by the student, the academic advisor or sponsoring faculty member, and the internship supervisor or apprenticeship mentor. Internships and apprenticeships are conducted on a pass/fail or credit/no credit system. Grades are not given for these experiences. The Internship and Career Services Director is located in the Student Success Center, next to Student Accounts. Office hours are Monday–Friday, 8 am–4 pm. Journey Home Ascendium Apprenticeships Internships Independent Study IAIA Student Chelsea A. Benally (Navajo) The Journey Home Apprenticeship and Internship program is open to Tribally enrolled IAIA BFA-seeking students who have not yet graduated. Students from all degree programs are encouraged to apply. Grant funding is for tribally enrolled students who reside in or where apprenticeships/internships are taking place in the following regions: Pacific Northwest (Oregon, Washington, British Columbia, or Alaska) Upper Midwest (Minnesota, Wisconsin, North Dakota, South Dakota) Southwest (Arizona, New Mexico, Southern California) Idaho and Montana What Makes the Journey Home Internship Special? Students applying for an apprenticeship are placed in a direct one-on-one mentoring relationship with an elder, a master artist, or a cultural expert willing to share their expertise and experience. The student works alongside the mentor on a specific project or undertaking. Through observation and direct involvement, students will become familiar with new artistic processes, cultural practices, and related approaches. Mentors will confirm the completion of hours and provide feedback. A $1,000 dollar stipend will be paid to mentors who participate in the apprenticeship program. Students applying for an internship will work at a host site of their choice. The internship enhances opportunities for students by combining experiential learning with service to Native communities. Students report to their supervisors and meet workplace expectations to earn academic credit. Supervisors will confirm student completion of hours and provide feedback. Students are not required to complete a for-credit apprenticeship or internship. Students should consult their Academic Advisor to determine whether it can be used toward their degree program. Eight apprenticeship or internship opportunities will be available for the 2026 summer session. The paid apprenticeship/internship provides selected students with the following stipend amounts: Tuition for a 3-credit course: $615 Travel: $2,500 Accommodations: $3,000 Stipend: $2,000 Supplies: $500 Listed stipend amounts are before tax, tuition, and other deductions. The funds are distributed to students bi-weekly throughout the apprenticeship/internship. Internship and Apprenticeship Academic Requirements: Complete 120 contact hours with the host site or mentor. Commit to bi-weekly check-ins with the Internship/Apprenticeship Coordinator and their cohort. Students will keep a weekly journal about their learning experience and process. Students will submit a 10–15-minute presentation on their experience at the end of the internship or apprenticeship. All class requirements must be met if students are enrolled in a for-credit class. Students will participate in an IAIA community presentation within one year following the internship/apprenticeship. How to Apply Before applying for the apprenticeship, students should identify a Tribally enrolled artist they would like to work with and confirm that the artist is willing to accept an apprentice. Students must identify the type of work they will be performing during the apprenticeship. Before applying for an internship, students should identify a host site and a supervisor they will be working for. Students must identify the type of work they will be performing during the internship. Journey Home Internship and Apprenticeship Application Link Application Submission Dates: March 30–April 26, 2026 Summer Session Dates: June 1–July 24, 2026 Selection is made by committee. For any technical difficulties or questions you may encounter with this application, please contact Angelica Gallegos at agallegos@iaia.edu . Ascendium paid internships benefit students, colleges and employers. Internship Eligibility Pays $10 per hour for 20 hours a week. Students must apply for the Internship and if selected, fill-out required internship forms available at the Internship and Career Services Director’s office. Eligible students must be currently enrolled, full-time, undergraduate, degree-seeking meeting satisfactory academic progress requirements and show financial need. Students that already hold a student worker or Work-Study position will not be eligible for this paid internship award. IAIA Staff and Faculty can hire a student for the paid internship (not currently a student worker or Work-Study position) and must submit the required paperwork to the Internship and Career Services Director at careers@iaia.edu. Open to organizations, galleries, film industry, museums that can provide a meaningful internship experience for Cinematic Arts & Technology, Creative Writing, Indigenous Liberal Studies, Museum Studies, or Studio Arts majors. Student can select their own site and setup their interview with site. Or contact the Internship and Career Director who can assist with the contact and coordination of site selection or site setup process. Note: Student may not begin internship until all hiring documents are complete through IAIA Human Resources. Participating Internship Hosts Previous and current internship hosts include Patricia Michaels (PM Waterlily), Flowering Tree Permaculture Institute, IAIA Indigenous Liberal Studies Department, Balzer Contemporary Edge Gallery, IAIA Library, New Mexico State Film Office, New Mexico History Museum, Ralph T. Coe Center for the Arts, SWAIA, Santa Fe Independent Film Festival, Duchy Films, Wheeler Museum, and Meow Wolf. For more information, please contact Internship and Career Services Director at (505) 424-2319 or careers@iaia.edu. Apprenticeships place students in a direct one-on-one mentoring relationship with an elder, a master artist, or a cultural expert who is willing to share his or her expertise and experience with a student. The student is to provide assistance to the mentor on a specific project or undertaking. In this way, through observation and direct involvement, students will become familiar with new artistic processes, cultural practices and related approaches. All students keep a journal to document their learning. Each student will write a final reflective paper. Mentors will complete an evaluation commenting on student learning through this experience. Over four years, students may enroll in two levels of apprenticeships for variable credit. Students may enroll in 1-6 credit apprenticeships. Apprenticeship I 295 Apprenticeship II 495 Apprenticeships are available in the following programs: MUSM295, MUSM 495 Museum Studies for one-on-one learning with an independent curator or related project. ARTS295, ARTS495 Studio Arts for one-on-one learning with a master artist or craftsperson. Any of the following prefixes may be used: CERA, JEWL, PRTM, SCUP, PHOT, PTNG. IDST295, IDST495, Indigenous Studies for one-on-one learning from a tribally-acknowledged expert. NMAD295, NMAD495 New Media Arts for one-on-one learning with an independent media artist or graphic artist. CRWR295, CRWR495 Creative Writing for one-on-one learning with a writer. Apprenticeship App Internships place students within existing organizations to learn directly from fellow employees, volunteers, clients and others. Internships provide valuable hands-on real world opportunities for student learning to deepen and strengthen classroom instruction and prepare students for professional careers in their chosen fields. Students report to workplace supervisors and fulfill workplace expectations to receive academic credit. All students keep a journal to document their learning. They will write a final reflective paper. Workplace supervisors will complete an evaluation form commenting on student’s learning in the workplace. Over their four years, students may enroll in two levels of internships for variable credit. Students may enroll in 1 to 6 credit internships. Internship I 290 (Prerequisite: 2nd year standing) Internship II 490 (Prerequisite: 3rd year standing) Internships are available in the following programs: MUSM290, MUSM490 Museum Studies-for work in galleries or museums ARTS290, ARTS490 Studio Arts for work in galleries and arts organizations any of the following prefixes may be used: CERA, JEWL, PRTM, SCUP, PHOT, PTNG. IDST290, IDST490 Indigenous Studies for work in tribal organizations and institutions serving Native peoples. ASG officers may choose to receive internship credit for their work. NMAD290, NMAD490 New Media Arts for work in graphic design, web design or moving image media. CRWR290, CRWR490 Creative Writing for work in publications, public relations, publishing, journalism, theater, or performing arts organizations. For Credit App An independent study provides an opportunity for an upper level student to work individually in a self-designed study under the guidance and direction of a fulltime faculty member. A student may not use more than three independent studies or a total of 9 credits toward the completion of a degree. An independent study does not satisfy general education requirements. Independent Study App Campus Security (505) 702-4274 • Anonymous Safety Report Institute of American Indian Arts (IAIA) 83 Avan Nu Po Road, Santa Fe NM 87508 • (505) 424-2300 IAIA Museum of Contemporary Native Arts (MoCNA) 108 Cathedral Place, Santa Fe NM 87501 • (505) 983-8900 Land Acknowledgement—The Institute of American Indian Arts (IAIA) respectfully acknowledges it is located on the traditional Puebloan lands of the Tanoan and Keres speaking Peoples. We honor and thank them for their graciousness as stewards of the land. © 2026 Institute of American Indian Arts Apply Today Tuition & Fees Catalog Give Home Portal Accessibility Emergency Communications Privacy Status Subscribe Title IX Contact Chat with us ,\npowered by LiveChat"
